ust castling directly.\n\nAmong these, **K: e1-g1** stands out as it secures the king, activates the rook via connection, and takes advantage of the fact that Black’s king is exposed on f7 — a sign of vulnerability.\n\nReason:  \nCastling is urgent and advantageous. It improves king safety, completes development, and prepares for central or kingside play. Black has moved their king early (KBF1 on f7), indicating potential instability. We should capitalize by securing our own king and preparing for an initiative. No immediate threat prevents castling (no checks, no pieces between), and the path is clear.\n\nAnswer: K: e1-g1"}
