Wait: bishop on d5 controls diagonal: d5-e4-f3-g2 and d5-e6-f7-g8. So f5 is **not** on that diagonal.\n\nSo after **Bd5+**, king on f6 is in check from a diagonal (c4-d5-e6-f7-g8) — so yes, the bishop checks along that diagonal.\n\nKing moves:\n- e5 — possible? e5 is empty. Is it attacked? Currently no. So Ke5 is possible.\n- g5 — g5 is empty? Yes. But after ...Kg5, White can play Qh5#? But we don’t have a queen out yet. But we could play h4+ or Nh3+ later. But not immediate.\n\nBut more importantly: **after Bd5+, Black is in serious trouble**: exposed king, and White gains tempo.\n\nOther options like P: a2-a4 are quiet and don’t exploit the weak king.\n\nAlso, note that **N: b2** is a knight on b2 (NBF1), but it's not listed as having moves — so probably no knight moves available.\n\nThe most aggressive and promising move is **B: c4-d5**, delivering check and increasing pressure on the exposed black king.\n\nReason:  \nThe black king on f6 is highly exposed, and White should act quickly before Black manages to castle or improve king safety. The move **B: c4-d5** delivers check, forces Black into a difficult decision, and increases pressure on the center. It develops a piece with tempo and may lead to rapid attack. Other moves are passive in comparison.\n\nAnswer: B: c4-d5"}
