----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: a1-b1, R: a1-c1, R: a1-d1, R: a1-e1, R: a1-f1, P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, B: d5-c4, B: d5-b3, B: d5-e4, B: d5-f3, B: d5-c6, B: d5-b7, B: d5-e6, B: d5-f7, B: d5-g8, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Evaluation:  \nLooking at the current position, Black\'s king is on f6, unusually exposed and already somewhat trapped in the center. White has a bishop on d5, which is actively placed and attacking key central squares. The Black king is currently under no direct threat, but several tactical motifs are possible.  \n\nKey observations:\n- The bishop on d5 exerts pressure on the long diagonal, especially toward h1–a8. Notably, it eyes the square f7, which is only defended by the Black king.\n- Black has no pieces near f7, and the pawn on g7 has advanced, weakening the kingside.\n- If White can increase pressure quickly, there may be a mating net forming.\n- The move **B: d5-f7** would capture a pawn with check, forcing the Black king to move (likely to g8 or e8), while opening lines for further attack.\n- After **Bxf7+**, if Black recaptures with the king (Kxf7), then the king remains exposed and White can follow up with Qd1–h5+, potentially leading to a strong attack.\n\nOther moves like pawn advances (a4, c4, g4) are slow and don’t exploit the current vulnerability of Black’s king. Castling (R: a1-b1 or K: g1-h1) is not actually valid castling notation — "K: g1-h1" is just a king move, and since the king has already moved (KWF1), castling is not allowed anyway.\n\nThe most aggressive and promising move is **B: d5-f7**, delivering check and targeting material while disrupting Black’s coordination.\n\nReason:  \nCapturing on f7 with the bishop delivers check, forces the Black king to move, and potentially opens the position for a rapid attack. This is consistent with principles of fast development, king exposure, and tactical aggression when the opponent’s king is stuck in the center.\n\nAnswer: B: d5-f7'}
